---
// Rewritten, and corrected. The previous version said the Fundamentals
// explainers were ones "I put together myself" that "carry my own
// name", in explicit contrast to Scout. That was not true — a model
// drafts those too — and it was the one claim on this site a reader
// could reasonably feel misled by. The full authorship story now lives
// at /colophon; this page states the shape of it and links there.
//
// It also absorbs the old "Welcome to securesein" post, which was a
// site announcement wearing a blog post's clothes.
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHeader from "../components/PageHeader.astro";
import { SITE, SECTIONS } from "../consts";
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Who writes this, what the three sections are, and exactly how much of it a model drafted."
  reading={true}
>
  <div class="container">
    <PageHeader
      title="About"
      standfirst="Applied AI, enterprise mobility, and the Microsoft ecosystem — from hands-on practice, not press releases."
    />

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I'm {SITE.author}. I work in enterprise device management: Intune,
        Android Enterprise, Apple management, the machinery that decides what
        thirty thousand company phones and laptops are allowed to do. That is
        the angle here. There is no shortage of blogs explaining what a new
        model can do; there are very few asking what happens when it has to be
        rolled out to a managed fleet, under policy, by people who will be
        paged if it breaks.
      </p>

      <h2>How much of this is written by a model</h2>
      <p>
        Nearly all of the sentences. That is true of the news items, and it is
        also true of the long explainers and the deep dives — which the old
        version of this page implied were hand-written, and they are not.
      </p>
      <p>
        What differs between them is how much of my judgement went in. On the
        news items: none, in the sense that nobody reads them before they
        publish. On everything else: I choose the subject, set the angle, supply
        the source material, check the claims that carry weight, and rewrite
        what comes back wrong. Every post says which of those things actually
        happened to it, in a box directly under the title, generated from a
        record rather than written by hand.
      </p>
      <p>
        The long version — which models, what I verify, what I don't, and what
        can still go wrong — is on the <a href="/colophon/">colophon</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>How Scout works</h2>
      <p>
        Scout is the pipeline behind the News section. It is not a person and
        it is not a persona; it is a few hundred lines of Python on a schedule.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>It reads a list of RSS feeds every few hours.</li>
        <li>
          It summarises what is new and messages the summaries to me on
          Telegram.
        </li>
        <li>
          I flag the ones worth writing up — sometimes with a one-line steer
          about the angle, sometimes not.
        </li>
        <li>
          A cloud model drafts a full post from the source article, usually
          within the hour.
        </li>
        <li>
          It publishes. Automatically. <strong>Nobody reads it first — not
          me.</strong> Every one links back to the article it came from.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        What is <em>not</em> reviewed: the facts, the framing, the numbers, the
        conclusion. My only editorial act is choosing which item gets written
        up at all. If a News post here matters to you, open its source and check
        it.
      </p>

      <h2>The three sections</h2>
      <dl class="lanes">
        {SECTIONS.map((section) => (
          <div class="lane">
            <dt><a href={section.href}>{section.label}</a></dt>
            <dd>{section.description}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>
      <p>
        Cutting across all three are the <a href="/topics/">topics</a> — seven
        subjects, so a two-minute news item and a twenty-minute teardown on the
        same thing end up on the same shelf. A few of those shelves are nearly
        empty on purpose: they are where this blog is meant to grow.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        If something here is wrong, I would rather know. How corrections are
        handled is on the <a href="/colophon/">colophon</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose { margin-top: 1rem; padding-bottom: 2rem; }
  .lanes { display: grid; gap: 0; }
  .lane {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.15rem;
    padding: 0.7rem 0 0.7rem 0.9rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .lane dt { font-weight: 700; }
  .lane dd { margin: 0; color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.95rem; }
</style>
